# 2D Copper Tetrahydroxyquinone Conductive Metal-Organic Framework for Selective CO2 Electrocatalysis at Low Overpotentials

## abstract
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are promising materials for electrocatalysis; however, lack of electrical conductivity in the majority of existing MOFs limits their effective utilization in the field. Herein, an excellent catalytic activity of a 2D copper (Cu)-based conductive MOF, copper tetrahydroxyquinone (Cuuf8ffTHQ), is reported for aqueous CO2 reduction reaction (CO2 RR) at low overpotentials. It is revealed that Cuuf8ffTHQ nanoflakes (NFs) with an average lateral size of 140 nm exhibit a negligible overpotential of 16 mV for the activation of this reaction, a high current density of ≈173 mA cm-2 at -0.45 V versus RHE, an average Faradaic efficiency (F.E.) of ≈91% toward CO production, and a remarkable turnover frequency as high as ≈20.82 s-1 . In the low overpotential range, the obtained CO formation current density is more than 35 and 25 times higher compared to state-of-the-art MOF and MOF-derived catalysts, respectively. The operando Cu K-edge X-ray absorption near edge spectroscopy and density functional theory calculations reveal the existence of reduced Cu (Cu+ ) during CO2 RR which reversibly returns to Cu2+ after the reaction. The outstanding CO2 catalytic functionality of conductive MOFs (c-MOFs) can open a way toward high-energy-density electrochemical systems.

2D Copper Tetrahydroxyquinone Conductive Metal–Organic Framework for Selective $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ Electrocatalysis at Low Overpotentials
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are promising materials for electrocatalysis; however, lack of electrical conductivity in the majority of existing MOFs limits their effective utilization in the field. Herein, an excellent catalytic activity of a 2D copper (Cu)-based conductive MOF, copper tetrahydroxyquinone $(C u-T H Q)$ , is reported for aqueous $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ reduction reaction $(C O_{2}R R)$ at low overpotentials. It is revealed that $\mathsf{c}_{\mathsf{u}}$ THQ nanoflakes (NFs) with an average lateral size of $140\:\mathsf{n m}$ exhibit a negligible overpotential of $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{16}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{m}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{V}}$ for the activation of this reaction, a high current density of ${\approx}173\ m A\ c m^{-2}$ at −0.45 V versus RHE, an average Faradaic efficiency (F.E.) of $\approx91\%$ toward CO production, and a remarkable turnover frequency as high as ${\approx}20.82~{\ s}^{-1}$ . In the low overpotential range, the obtained CO formation current density is more than 35 and 25 times higher compared to state-of-the-art MOF and MOFderived catalysts, respectively. The operando Cu K-edge X-ray absorption near edge spectroscopy and density functional theory calculations reveal the existence of reduced $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{C u^{+}})$ during $C O_{2}R R$ which reversibly returns to ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ after the reaction. The outstanding $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ catalytic functionality of conductive MOFs (c-MOFs) can open a way toward high-energy-density electrochemical systems.
Growing worldwide energy demand and rising consumption rate of fossil fuels could lead to catastrophic consequences facing modern society. To conquer the upcoming energy crisis and to develop sustainable energy technologies, discovery of new materials with outstanding functionalities is necessary. In this regard, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are ideal materials due to their extraordinary structural versatility, synthetic tunability, ultrahigh porosity, and high surface area.[1–6] MOFs are crystalline structures composed of inorganic metal ions and organic ligands that can be synthesized through a bottom-up assembly approach under solvothermal conditions. To date, numerous MOFs are used extensively for bulk applications such as heterogeneous catalysis, gaseous energy storage $\left\langle\mathrm{H}_{2}\right\rangle$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4})$ , and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture, separations, and reduction reaction.[7–13] However, the insulating nature (lack of electrical conductivity) of the majority of the existing MOFs restricts their successful utilization in electrocatalysis, which is central to applications in the field of energy storage and generation.[14,15] In order to mitigate this challenge, molecular engineering strategies have been recently employed to harness the reticular chemistry and robust tunability of classical MOFs to develop conductive MOFs (c-MOFs), which demonstrate both porosity and electrical conductivity.[14–17] While the c-MOFs have recently shown a great promise in hydrogen evolution reaction (HER),[18,19] oxygen reduction and evolution reaction,[15,19,20] and supercapacitors,[21,22] there still remains a lack of highly efficient c-MOF-based catalysts to be utilized in core electrochemical reactions such as electrochemical $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction reaction $(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR})$ .
Herein, we report the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}$ electrocatalytic activity of a 2D copper $\mathrm{(Cu)}$ -based c-MOF, copper tetrahydroxyquinone (CuTHQ), at low overpotentials using a hybrid electrolyte comprising of $1\ \mathrm{~\textmu~}$ choline chloride $\mathrm{(C_{5}H_{14}C I N O)}$ and $1\textbf{M}$ potassium hydroxide (KOH). This hybrid electrolyte was selected due to its superior ionic conductivity, high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ solubility, and stability for the ${\mathrm{CO}}_{2}{\mathrm{RR}}$ .[23] The bulk CuTHQ with the chemical formula of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{6})_{2}$ and the electrical conductivity of $\approx1.5\times10^{-7}\ \mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ was synthesized based on a method previously reported (see Section S1 in the Supporting Information and ref. [17]) and exfoliated into 2D nanoflakes (NFs) via liquid-phase exfoliation technique for an increased surface-to-volume ratio (see Section S2 in the Supporting Information). The successful synthesis of 2D structures was confirmed by our comprehensive characterization study, including energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX), high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM), X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS), and atomic force microscopy (AFM). Moreover, operando Cu K-edge X-ray absorption near edge spectroscopy (XANES) and density functional theory (DFT) calculations shed light on identifying the active sites and chemical changes during the ${\mathrm{CO}}_{2}{\mathrm{RR}}$ .
Figure 1a shows the dispersed NFs of $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ THQ in isopropyl alcohol (IPA) with a distinct color after liquid-phase exfoliation (see the “Experimental Section”). A typical AFM image of CuTHQ NFs deposited on $\mathrm{Si}/\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ substrate is shown in Figure  1b. The statistical thickness analysis of 40 randomly selected flakes revealed an average thickness of $10.1\pm6.7~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 1c). Figure 1d shows an average lateral size of $140\pm52\mathrm{nm}$ for one batch of CuTHQ NFs obtained by dynamic light scattering (DLS) (see Section S3 in the Supporting Information for the rest of DLS and AFM results). To examine the structural stability of c-MOF NFs after exfoliation, HRTEM images were obtained. The HRTEM images (Figure 1e–g) clearly show elliptical pores, smaller than $1\mathrm{nm}$ with a honeycomb arrangement along [001] with $d_{110}=11.7\mathring\mathrm{A}$ and $d_{020}=11.0\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , which corresponds well with the AB stacking model of CuTHQ (calculated $d_{110}=11.2\mathrm{~\AA~}$ and $d_{020}=10.9$ Å).[17] Elemental mapping with energy-dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) indicates a homogeneous C, O, and Cu distribution in the exfoliated CuTHQ sample (see Figure S3 in the Supporting Information). These observations confirm that the crystal structure was well preserved in exfoliated CuTHQ samples. XPS was also performed on the bulk and exfoliated CuTHQ sample deposited on gas diffusion electrodes (GDEs). Full energy spectral results shown in Figure  1h indicate the presence of Cu, C, and O species for both bulk and
2D CuTHQ NFs (see Section S5 in the Supporting Information). Moreover, a small amount of N species is observed in the XPS spectra due to the synthesis process.[17] An extra F peak is also observed in the XPS survey spectrum of the CuTHQ NFs, which is due to the polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE) present on the GDE substrate.[24] Overall, our characterization results confirm the structural integrity of c-MOF after exfoliation.
To evaluate the catalytic performance of 2D c-MOF, linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) experiments were performed for different particle sizes. The LSV experiments were carried out in a three-electrode setup containing $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ saturated $1\mathrm{~}\mathrm{~u~}$ choline chloride and $1\textbf{M}$ KOH at a scan rate of $50\ \mathrm{mV\s^{-1}}$ .[23] To prepare the working electrodes, the synthesized c-MOF NFs were deposited on GDE substrates (see the “Experimental Section”). $\mathrm{Ag/AgNO_{3}}$ and $\mathrm{Pt}$ meshes were used as reference and counter electrodes, respectively (see Sections S6 and S7 in the Supporting Information). The LSV results shown in Figure 2a indicate that CuTHQ NFs with the average particle size of $140~\mathrm{nm}$ delivered the highest current density of $\mathrm{\tilde{\s}}173\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\cm}^{-2}$ at $-0.45\mathrm{~V~}$ versus RHE. At the same potential, the current densities for average particle sizes of 300 and $600\ \mathrm{nm}$ are ${\approx}146.3$ and ${\approx}96~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , respectively.
To detect the gas products and measure the onset potentials of the corresponding reactions, LSV experiments were performed in a two-compartment cell at a low scan rate of $1\mathrm{mVs^{-1}}$ with real-time product analysis using differential electrochemical mass spectroscopy (DEMS) (Figure 2b). During the DEMS online measurement, CO was observed as a major product, and no other carbon-based gas phase products were detected. We obtained an onset potential of $-0.126\mathrm{~V~}$ versus RHE for CO formation, suggesting a low overpotential of $16~\mathrm{mV}$ compared to the thermodynamic potential of $-0.11\mathrm{~V~}$ for this reaction[25] (see Sections S8 and S9 in the Supporting Information). This value is so far the lowest onset potential reported for ${\mathrm{CO}}_{2}{\mathrm{RR}}$ compared to the state-of-the-art catalysts[25–33] (also see Table S2 in the Supporting Information). With the overpotential continuously increasing, CO production faced an increase in the tested potential range. $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production was also negligible and remained stable during the experiment.
To gain more insight into the gas product formation during ${\mathrm{CO}}_{2}{\mathrm{RR}}_{3}$ , 1 h chronoamperometry experiments were performed at three different potentials in the range of $-0.135$ and $-0.43\mathrm{~V~}$ versus RHE. Current density profiles for these experiments show no more than $20\%$ decrease compared to the current densities observed in LSV experiment, which is attributed to the charging current (capacitive behavior) in the LSV measurements (see Figure S8 in the Supporting Information). DEMS was also used in each experiment to detect the gas products. Similar to the LSV experiment, no carbon-based gas products were observed other than CO (see the DEMS results in Figure S9 in the Supporting Information). The corresponding Faradaic efficiency (F.E.) of CO and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , as depicted in the inset of Figure 2b, is on average ${\approx}91\%$ at the potential window from $-0.135$ to $-0.43\mathrm{\DeltaV}$ versus RHE. Furthermore, the average F.E. for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ formation was measured to be negligible $(<1\%)$ in this potential window. The remaining F.E. is attributed to the loss in the system and/or small percentage of possible liquid products. These results indicate that ${\mathrm{CO}}_{2}{\mathrm{RR}}$ with CuTHQ NFs is favorable toward CO production in the $-0.135$ to $-0.43\mathrm{~V~}$ versus RHE range.
The achieved current density with CuTHQ NFs (Figure 2a) is the highest value ever reported for the MOF and MOF-derived catalysts in ${\mathrm{CO}}_{2}{\mathrm{RR}}$ , especially with selectivity toward CO formation (see Table S2 in the Supporting Information).[34–42] In particular, the CO formation current density achieved by
CuTHQ NFs at $-0.43\mathrm{~V~}$ versus RHE is ${\approx}25$ times higher than that of bimetallic $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Zn}$ zeolitic imidazolate framework-derived $\mathrm{Co-N}_{2}$ at the potential of $-0.48{\mathrm{~V~}}$ versus $\mathrm{RHE}^{[38]}$ and ${\approx}37{}$ times higher than the value reported for porphyrin-based MOF of PCN$222\mathrm{(Fe)/C}$ at $-0.45\mathrm{~V~}$ versus RHE.[42] In addition, Table S3 (Supporting Information) compares the electrochemical performance of $\mathrm{cu}$ THQ NFs with a number of Cu-based catalysts with CO production. For instance, the CO formation current density obtained by CuTHQ NFs at $-0.43\mathrm{~V~}$ versus RHE is ${\approx}38{\$ and ${\approx}35$ times higher than the values achieved by the state-of-the-art Cu-based catalysts of $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ nanosheets at $-0.5{\mathrm{~V~}}$ versus $\mathrm{RHE}^{[43]}$ and annealed Cu foam at $-0.45\mathrm{\DeltaV}$ versus RHE,[44] respectively. Moreover, CuTHQ NFs greatly outperform noble metal (e.g., Ag and Au)[25,30–33] and other nonprecious catalysts.[26–29]
DFT calculations revealed that Cu species together with neighboring O species in the catalyst are the active sites contributing to the ${\mathrm{CO}}_{2}{\mathrm{RR}}$ (see the DFT discussion below). Therefore, to determine the turnover numbers (TONs) of CO production per mole of Cu, we measured the total Cu loading on the cathode using inductively coupled plasma optical emission absorption spectroscopy (ICP-OES, see Section S13 in the Supporting Information). Our experiments revealed that ${\approx}1.6\times10^{-7}$ moles of Cu exist per square centimeter of the cathode electrode. The TON values of CO production were then calculated from chronoamperometry experiments assuming that all Cu species in the catalyst are electrochemically active (see Section S14 in the Supporting Information). Figure 2c shows an increasing trend of TON values with the applied potentials where at the potential of $-0.43\mathrm{~V~}$ versus RHE an exceptionally high value of ${\approx}7.49\times10^{4}$ was obtained after $^{1\mathrm{h}}$ . CO formation turnover frequency (TOF) was also found to b $\mathsf{s}\approx20.82\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ at $-0.43\mathrm{~V~}$ versus RHE (inset of Figure 2c), which is by far the highest TOF reported for ${\mathrm{CO}}_{2}{\mathrm{RR}}$ in low overpotential range (see Table S2 in the Supporting Information).[26–30,32,33,45,46]
The stability of CuTHQ catalyst was investigated using XPS experiments over an extended $^\textrm{\scriptsize7h}$ chronoamperometry experiment at the controlled potential of $-0.43\mathrm{~V~}$ versus RHE (see Figure S10 in the Supporting Information). Figure  2d shows the $\mathrm{Cu}\ 2\mathrm{p}$ spectra of CuTHQ cathode before and after the electrochemical reaction. The results indicate that before the reaction, the CuTHQ is composed of $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ . After the ${\mathrm{CO}}_{2}{\mathrm{RR}}$ ， two $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ main peaks in the pristine cathode located at ${\approx}934.4\$ and ${\approx}954.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ were shifted to lower binding energies of 933.7 and $953.6\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively. The binding energies of both pristine and cycled samples were within the range reported in the NIST database for a formal oxidation state of $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ .[47] While the pristine state is closer to reported values for $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}{-}\mathrm{O}^{2-}$ bonds in compounds like hydroxides and carbonates in the literature,[48,49] the cycled state is more consistent with $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}{-}\mathrm{O}^{2-}$ bonds in oxides.[50–52] However, metallic $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ and $\mathrm{Cu^{+}{-}O^{2-}}$ species are typically observed at least $1~\mathrm{eV}$ lower in energy than any signals observed here.[47,50] Therefore, the peak shift observed after cycling does not signify reduction, but can rather be attributed to a change in the chemical environment of $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ after electrocatalysis.[51] Furthermore, two $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ satellite peaks observed in the pristine cathode faced a significant decrease in intensity due to the reduction reaction, but they remained after cycling. The obtained results confirmed the stability of catalyst and were consistent with the XPS results after $^\textrm{\scriptsize7h}$ chronoamperometry. Moreover, ex situ $\mathrm{Cu}~\mathrm{L}_{3}$ -edge X-ray absorption spectroscopy (XAS) experiments were performed on the pristine state and after cycling for 1 and $^{7\mathrm{~h~}}$ , respectively (Figure  2e). The $\mathrm{Cu\L_{3}}.$ edge spectrum results from the dipole allowed transition of Cu $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ electrons into empty $\mathrm{Cu}3\mathrm{d}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{O}2\mathrm{p}$ hybridized states, if present, with a weaker transition allowed for Cu 4s–O 2p states at higher energy. The pristine CuTHQ NFs show a sharp peak at $930.85\ \mathrm{eV},$ with weaker signals at 934.1 and $937.9{\mathrm{~eV}},$ which are broadly consistent with reported peak positions of a formal $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ state.[53] The peak around $931\mathrm{eV}$ arises from the $\mathrm{2p}_{3/2}\mathrm{\rightarrow3d^{9}}$ transition,[53] which is absent in $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{0}$ because they both have a filled d shell; both have peaks near $933.7\mathrm{eV},$ absent in our data.[54] After the reaction, the most intense peak shifts to lower energy, by $0.2\mathrm{-}0.3\mathrm{eV}.$ The persistence of this $\mathrm{2p}_{3/2}\mathrm{\rightarrow3d^{9}}$ transition again supports a dominant formal $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ state, with the absence of any obvious signals indicating the concurrent presence $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{0}$ . The slight shift to lower energy with higher reaction time suggests a change in the chemical environment of $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ as a result of catalysis, in agreement with the XPS results. The position of the $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ $\mathrm{L}_{3}$ -edge peaks is known to be very sensitive to the nature of its covalent interaction with different ligands.[55]
Additionally, a long-term chronoamperometry experiment (24 h) was performed for CuTHQ NFs at the potential of $-0.43\mathrm{V}$ versus RHE, and the stability of catalyst was further confirmed via XPS (see Figures S11 and S12 in the Supporting Information). Moreover, nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectroscopy was utilized to evaluate the stability of the electrolyte. The obtained $^1\mathrm{H}$ and $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ NMR spectra confirm the stability of the choline chloride/KOH electrolyte after the long-term chronoamperometry experiment (see Section S12 in the Supporting Information).
