The structures of clean ${\mathrm{Cu}}{\mathrm{-THQ}}$ surface and the key intermediates are shown in Figure $3\mathrm{a-c}$ . In $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, the first proton–electron transfer gives COOH on CuTHQ surface (see Equation  (1)). The COOH binds on O site next to the Cu atom via unsaturated C, as shown in Figure  3b. The second proton–electron transfer yields CO and water, as described in Equation (2). The CO binds on Cu site via the C atom with the perpendicular configuration (as depicted in Figure 3c). Due to the CO adsorption, the Cu atom is pulled out of plane as shown in the side view in the bottom panel in Figure 3c.
In summary, we found that c-MOFs are ideal catalytic candidates to drastically enhance the energy efficiency and product selectivity of ${\mathrm{CO}}_{2}{\mathrm{RR}}$ . In particular, we revealed that 2D CuTHQ c-MOF delivers record values of TOFs and TONs for CO formation at low overpotentials and exhibits a negligible overpotential for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction reaction. XANES and DFT results further supported the electrochemical results and provided more in-depth insight into the reaction mechanism. These interesting findings can open a new way for further advances in core electrocatalytic reactions with applications in energy storage and conversion systems.
## Experimental Section
Cathode Preparation: CuTHQ NFs were synthesized using a liquidphase exfoliation method. About $30~\mathsf{m g}$ of the $C u\mathrm{-}T H Q$ powder was dispersed in $72m L$ of IPA. Ultrasonication (Vibra Cell Sonics 130 W) was used for $4{\cdot}6\ h$ followed by centrifugation for $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ at 2000–3000 rpm to obtain the corresponding reduced lateral sizes of NFs. For cathode preparation, $200~\mathsf{m g}$ of CuTHQ NFs was deposited on $\mathsf{1}\mathsf{c m}^{2}$ of GDE (Sigracet 25 BC, purchased from FuelCellsEtc). The coated substrate was dried overnight in an argon filled glove box.
DLS Experiments: The Malvern Zetasizer Nano ZSP system was used to measure the lateral size of NFs dispersed in IPA at $25^{\circ}C$ . The instrument was operated with a $10\ m\forall V$ semiconductor laser with $633~\mathsf{n m}$ emissions.
AFM Experiments: A Bruker ICON Dimension was used to obtain the topography maps of drop-cast flakes and the statistical flake thickness distributions. After drop-casting the CuTHQ NFs on silicon chips, the substrates were carefully washed with acetone, IPA, and deionized water.
TEM Experiments: Samples for TEM observation were diluted in IPA. A droplet of the suspension was transferred onto a carbon-coated copper grid. High-angle annular dark-field (HAADF) STEM imaging and EDS mapping were performed on a JEOL JEM2100 microscope, and operated at $200~\mathsf{k V}$ (Cs: $1.0\ \mathsf{m m}$ , point resolution: $0.23\ \mathsf{n m}$ ). TEM images were recorded with a Gatan Ultrascan CCD camera (resolution $2048\times2048$ pixels).
$x P S$ Experiments: A Thermo-Scientific ESCALAB 250Xi instrument was utilized to perform XPS experiments. Thermo Avantage software was used to analyze and process each element's data.
Electrolyte Preparation: Choline chloride was purchased from Sigma– Aldrich. KOH was purchased from Fisher-Scientific. The solutions were mixed to the desired concentration using Barnstead NANO pure water $(78.2\mathsf{M}\Omega$ and $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ ).
Electrochemical Experiments: The CuTHQ-coated GDE was used as the working electrode. ${\sf A g}/{\sf A g N O}_{3}$ (with 0.1 m ${\mathsf{A g N O}}_{3}$ in 1 m KOH and 1 m choline chloride as the filling solution) and platinum mesh (surface area: $0.48~\mathsf{c m}^{2}$ ) were used as reference and counter electrodes, respectively. First, ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ gas was bubbled for ${\approx}30$ min into the electrolyte along with stirring to minimize the mass transfer effect and complete the saturation. The ${\mathsf{p H}}$ of the electrolyte was measured to be ${\approx}7.6$ . LSV experiments were performed in a standard two-compartment electrochemical cell at a scan rate of $50\ m\vee\ s^{-1}$ . All potentials were converted to RHE (see Section S7 in the Supporting Information).
DEMS Experiments: Real-time analysis of the gas products from ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}{\mathsf{R R}}$ was carried out using DEMS (purchased from Hiden Analytical). In order to calibrate the gas species ( $\mathrm{\Pi}_{\mathrm{H}_{2}}$ and CO), the two-compartment cell was used as the working setup, and four gas mixtures of $1\%$ , $5\%$ , $10\%$ , and $20\%$ for each of the gas species ( $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}_{2}^{\mathsf{H}_{2}}$ and CO) in argon were chosen. The different gas mixtures were purchased from Praxair. For further details of gas product characterization and DEMS experiments, see Sections S8–S10 in the Supporting Information.
XANES Experiments: Cu K-edge XANES measurements were conducted between 8867 and $9270\ \mathrm{~eV}$ at beamline 9-BM and longer duration scans were recorded at beamline 10-BM at the Advanced Photon Source. The operando cell was built (Figure S14a, Supporting Information) with Kapton windows for X-ray passage, with a platinum wire as a counter electrode, ${\sf A g}/{\sf A g N O}_{3}$ as the reference electrode, and the $C u\mathrm{-}T H Q$ catalyst deposited on GDE was used as a working electrode. The electrolyte was the mixture of 1 m choline chloride and 1 m KOH. ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ gas was bubbled through the electrolyte at a slow rate to induce saturation. Measurements were performed in fluorescence mode using a Vortex-ME4 four-element silicon drift detector. For further details of Cu- $\mathsf{L}_{3}$ XAS experiments, see Section S16 in the Supporting Information. DFT Calculations: For details of DFT calculations, see Section S17 in the Supporting Information.
